// @SpringBootApplication = @SpringBootConfiguration + @EnableAutoConfiguration
//   + @ComponentScan. One annotation, one main method -> runnable app.
@SpringBootApplication
public class App {
    public static void main(String[] a) { SpringApplication.run(App.class, a); }
}

// A REST controller — Spring MVC, but Boot auto-configures Tomcat + MVC.
@RestController
class Hello {
    @GetMapping("/hello") String hi() { return "hi"; }
}

// How auto-configuration works: Boot sees spring-web on the classpath ->
//   it registers Tomcat + DispatcherServlet + JSON converter beans FOR YOU.
//   If a DataSource + a JDBC driver are present -> it configures a DataSource
//   from application.yml. You write NO wiring; you override only what differs.

// application.yml — externalized config overrides auto-config defaults.
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost/payments
    username: ${DB_USER}        # env var — no secrets in the jar
server:
  port: 8080                   # a default you can override

**ApplicationStartedEvent**
- Fires after context refresh completes
- Boot calls all `CommandLineRunner` and `ApplicationRunner` beans
- First point where every singleton bean is guaranteed to exist, every `@PostConstruct` has completed, and the server is bound

**Runner types and ordering**
- `CommandLineRunner` receives raw `String[] args`
- `ApplicationRunner` receives an `ApplicationArguments` object that parses option arguments (like `--server.port=8081`)
- `@Order` (lower value = higher priority) controls execution sequence when multiple runners exist

**Runner code example**

```java
@Component
@Order(1)
class DataSeedingRunner implements ApplicationRunner {
    private final OrderRepository orders;

    DataSeedingRunner(OrderRepository orders) { this.orders = orders; }

    @Override
    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) {
        if (args.containsOption("seed")) {
            orders.seedDefaultData();
        }
    }
}
```

**ApplicationReadyEvent**
- The signal that the application is fully initialized
- Health check endpoints like `/actuator/health/readiness` should return UP only after this event
- Maps to the Kubernetes readiness probe contract

**Failure semantics**
- If any runner throws, startup fails and `ApplicationFailedEvent` fires instead of `ApplicationReadyEvent`
- `ApplicationStartedEvent` = 'context is refreshed'
- `ApplicationReadyEvent` = 'everything succeeded; accept traffic'

**Use `@AutoConfiguration` (Boot 2.7+) over `@Configuration`**
- Dedicated annotation that signals participation in auto-configuration ordering
- Supports `after` and `before` attributes directly
- `@Configuration` works but loses these guarantees

**Always guard with `@ConditionalOnClass`**
- Without it, your auto-config throws `NoClassDefFoundError` in apps that don't use your library
- The cheapest filter — evaluated first by Spring's condition machinery

**Always guard `@Bean` methods with `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`**
- Lets consumers override your default by defining their own bean
- Without it, consumers get `BeanDefinitionOverrideException` on customization

**Pair with `@EnableConfigurationProperties` for typed config**
- Explicit binding: consumers see exactly which properties your auto-configuration activates
- Avoids accidental binding via `@ConfigurationPropertiesScan` on the consumer side

**Complete example**

```java
@AutoConfiguration(after = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
@ConditionalOnClass(MetricsService.class)
@EnableConfigurationProperties(MetricsProperties.class)
public class MetricsAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public MetricsService metricsService(MetricsProperties props) {
        return new MetricsService(props.endpoint(), props.interval());
    }

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(value = "metrics.scheduler.enabled",
                           havingValue = "true", matchIfMissing = true)
    public MetricsScheduler metricsScheduler(MetricsService svc) {
        return new MetricsScheduler(svc);
    }
}
```

**Ordering pitfalls**
- Two auto-configs with circular `after`/`before` declarations → `IllegalStateException: cycle detected` at startup
- Declare ordering only where there's a genuine dependency, not defensively

**Why `ApplicationContextRunner`**
- Builds a minimal context with just your auto-config — no full app startup
- Lets you simulate different classpath and property combinations
- Tests run in milliseconds

**Three tests every auto-config needs**

```java
class MetricsAutoConfigurationTest {

    private final ApplicationContextRunner runner =
        new ApplicationContextRunner()
            .withConfiguration(AutoConfigurations.of(MetricsAutoConfiguration.class));

    @Test
    void registersServiceWithDefaults() {
        runner.run(ctx -> {
            assertThat(ctx).hasSingleBean(MetricsService.class);
            assertThat(ctx.getBean(MetricsProperties.class).endpoint())
                .isEqualTo("http://localhost:9090");
        });
    }

    @Test
    void userOverrideWins() {
        runner.withBean(MetricsService.class,
                () -> new MetricsService("custom", Duration.ofSeconds(5)))
            .run(ctx -> assertThat(ctx.getBean(MetricsService.class).endpoint())
                .isEqualTo("custom"));
    }

    @Test
    void backsOffWhenClassMissing() {
        runner.withClassLoader(new FilteredClassLoader(MetricsService.class))
            .run(ctx -> assertThat(ctx).doesNotHaveBean(MetricsService.class));
    }
}
```

**Why test number 3 matters**
- Proves `@ConditionalOnClass` is wired to the right class
- Confirms the auto-config truly does not activate for consumers without your library
- The contract you make with consumers: 'we won't break your app if you don't use us'

**IDE metadata via the configuration processor**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-configuration-processor</artifactId>
    <optional>true</optional>
</dependency>
```

- Generates `META-INF/spring-configuration-metadata.json` at compile time
- Consumers typing `metrics.` in `application.yml` see `endpoint` and `interval` suggested with type and Javadoc
- Without the processor, consumer IDEs show 'unknown property' warnings

**`ApplicationListener` vs `@EventListener`**
- `ApplicationListener<E>` registered as a bean fires for events after the context is created
- `SpringApplication.addListeners(...)` or registration via `spring.factories` fires for events before the context exists
- `@EventListener` is bean-based — requires the context, can't see early events

**Pre-context registration**

```
# META-INF/spring.factories
org.springframework.context.ApplicationListener=\
  com.example.startup.EarlyConfigListener
```

```java
public class EarlyConfigListener implements ApplicationListener<ApplicationEnvironmentPreparedEvent> {
    @Override
    public void onApplicationEvent(ApplicationEnvironmentPreparedEvent event) {
        ConfigurableEnvironment env = event.getEnvironment();
        env.getPropertySources().addFirst(
            new MapPropertySource("vault", fetchSecretsFromVault())
        );
    }
}
```

**`EnvironmentPostProcessor` — the cleaner alternative**

```java
public class VaultSecretsEnvironmentPostProcessor implements EnvironmentPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public void postProcessEnvironment(ConfigurableEnvironment environment,
                                         SpringApplication application) {
        environment.getPropertySources().addFirst(
            new MapPropertySource("vault-secrets", loadSecrets())
        );
    }
}
```

```
# META-INF/spring.factories
org.springframework.boot.env.EnvironmentPostProcessor=\
  com.example.startup.VaultSecretsEnvironmentPostProcessor
```

**Use cases**
- Vault secret injection before any bean reads the environment
- Custom property formats (encrypted values, remote config)
- Conditional profile activation based on external state
- Slack/PagerDuty alerts on `ApplicationFailedEvent` before JVM exits

**Banner customization**
- Place `banner.txt` in `src/main/resources` — replaces the default Spring Boot ASCII art
- Placeholders: `${spring-boot.version}`, `${application.title}`, `${application.version}`, `${spring.application.name}`
- For dynamic content, implement `Banner` and pass via `SpringApplication.setBanner(...)`
- Disable entirely with `spring.main.banner-mode=off` for cleaner CI logs

**Operational value of the banner**
- Identifies the application name and version in aggregated logs
- Useful when many services log to the same aggregator and you need to spot which produced what

**`ApplicationContextInitializer`**
- Runs before the context is refreshed but after creation
- Used for adding property sources, setting active profiles programmatically, registering bean definitions early
- Registered via `spring.factories` under `ApplicationContextInitializer`

```java
public class ProfileFromEnvInitializer
        implements ApplicationContextInitializer<ConfigurableApplicationContext> {
    @Override
    public void initialize(ConfigurableApplicationContext ctx) {
        String region = System.getenv("AWS_REGION");
        if (region != null) {
            ctx.getEnvironment().addActiveProfile("region-" + region);
        }
    }
}
```

**Registration**

```
# META-INF/spring.factories
org.springframework.context.ApplicationContextInitializer=\
  com.example.startup.ProfileFromEnvInitializer
```
